Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 09/21/2023]
 1. Er = 205 keV: average of three resonance sterngths: Stegmueller et al. 1996 (direct), Sallaska et al. 2011 (direct), Fougeres et al. 2023 (indirect).
 2. Er = 275 keV: average of three resonance strenghts: Seuthe et al. 1990, Stegmueller et al. 1996, Sallaska et al. 2011.
 3. Er = 435, 583 keV: average of Seuthe et al. 1990 and Sallaska et al. 2011.
 4. Er = 482, 708, 750 keV: from Seuthe et al. 1990.
 5. Er = 44 keV: (2J+1)C2S from Schmidt et al. 1995; Gg from lifetime listed in ENSDF.
 6. Er = 68 keV: (2J+1)C2S from Schmidt et al. 1995; since Jenkins et al. 2013 assign 3/2+, only the ell=2 component in Schmidt is used.
 7. Er = 190 keV: Gp from resonance strength upper limit in Sallaska et al. 2011; Gg from lifetime in ENSDF; assumed p-wave resonance since Jenkins et al. 2006
    reports Jp = (9/2-) and ENSDF quotes 5/2-,7/2-.
 8. Er = 202 keV: Gp from resonance strength upper limit in Sallaska et al. 2011; Gg > 0.65 eV from lifetime upper limit in ENSDF.
 9. Er = 223 keV: T=3/2 (IAS) state; Gp from wg upper limits in Seuthe et al. 1990, Stegmueller et al. 1996, Sallaska et al. 2011; Gg is a guess.
10. Er = 3 keV: Gp from C2S upper limit in Schmidt et al. 1995; Gg is a guess.
11. Direct capture: not enough information on C2S of bound 23Mg levels is available; Seuthe et al. 1990 report S_DC = 18 keVb, estimated by assuming a dimensionless
    reduced proton width of 0.1 for all bound levels; in this mass range, S_DC ranges from 10 keVb (18O+p) to 56 keVb (25Mg+p); we will adopt 20 keVb with a factor
    of 3 uncertainty.
